 "critiqueNotes": "<p>The four critics agreed to a surprising degree. Écharpe and Semelle were in every critic&#x27;s top two, Porte-clés was third in three of the four orders, and Tir and Brassard were at the bottom in all four. The disagreement was about the middle: Panneau against Porte-clés, and how much credit Quatre Ombres earns for its idea. When a brand director, a teenager, a numbers reader and a strict rules reader agree this closely, it is evidence and not taste.</p><p>The defining pattern is that the idea and the object come apart. The most charged ideas sat on the weakest objects: the four floodlight shadows, the captain&#x27;s armband, the stencil numeral and the &#x27;top corner&#x27; line. The winners are objects people already own, collect and care about: the scarf, the boots, the keys. The lesson for the next round is to choose the object first and borrow the idea second. Several losers donate their best part: Pochoir&#x27;s cut-through 84, Lucarne&#x27;s top-corner beat, Lsaq&#x27;s sticker export, Panneau&#x27;s 24px token and its raise-the-board share, and Quatre Ombres&#x27; light for reveal campaigns.</p><p>The weakest habit is the tier ladder that is only a colour swap, which the brief explicitly ruled out. Pochoir rebuilt the FUT bronze-silver-gold ladder, and Tir, Brassard and Quatre Ombres change paint or light that nobody can see in a leaderboard row. Only Semelle (studs breaking the outline), Écharpe (the raised scarf) and Porte-clés (the carabiner) actually meet rule 2. A ladder that only a designer can read does not create status.</p><p>Compliance with the binding rules slipped in ways the critics only partly named. Rule 3 is broken by Pochoir (brass already at PRO) and, less obviously, by Semelle: its brass founder wheel sits on black at STADE and on carbon at CHAMPION, which is exactly the forbidden black-and-brass pairing. Rule 10 is broken by Semelle&#x27;s centred bust in a photo window. Rule 4 is broken by Écharpe and Lsaq, whose rows fall back to text or drop the ·26. Rule 8 is missed by the black and grey tokens of Lucarne, Panneau and Porte-clés. Rule 15 is missed by Lucarne, Tir and Brassard, where navy is still the default. Two concepts (Panneau, and Écharpe&#x27;s LEGEND fists) draw a single fixed skin tone for every user&#x27;s hand. A glove, a sleeve cuff or a crop solves that.</p><p>Data integrity defects are the cheapest to fix and the most damaging if they ship. Brassard sets stat values against the wrong labels in both scripts, Panneau&#x27;s dot-matrix PRO face reads 82/86/78 as B2/B6/7B, and Lucarne&#x27;s tier strip drops the stat labels. The product&#x27;s whole promise is honest numbers, so every refined card should be checked by reading the rasterised output at 1x, not the source.</p><p>Meaning risks deserve their own gate. Every strong concept carries a misreading that only audience culture reveals. A shown shoe sole is an insult in Morocco (Semelle), the board signals substitution or minute 84 (Panneau), an X means cancel or the X logo (Quatre Ombres), a teardrop key reads as riad or hotel (Porte-clés), and the hood inside a circle reads as an arched doorway (Lsaq, and Pochoir&#x27;s crest disc). That last one is systemic: the shared hood-up avatar becomes a tall black arch whenever it is cropped into a circle, so the fix belongs in MC.avatar(), by showing shoulders and the hood seam, not in each concept.</p><p>There were two surprises in the categories. The &#x27;safe&#x27; Porte-clés produced the most adult object and the best Founder mechanic in the pool, and the &#x27;youth&#x27; Panneau turned out to be the safest, most broadcast-like system. Moroccan relevance was mostly achieved by leaving clichés out rather than by putting anything in: only Écharpe (8.0) and Pochoir (6.0) score above 5, so the refinement round should add positive local signals (supporter years, neighbourhood-tournament craft, club-colour slots) rather than merely avoid zellige.</p><p>Against Codex, nothing resembles Stadium Architecture, Touchline or Stadium Pass. Écharpe sits thematically near Terrace and Panneau near Broadcast. That proximity is one more reason to prefer Porte-clés for the third slot, and a reason to keep stands and stadium architecture out of Écharpe&#x27;s share image.</p>",
